<header><p class="eyebrow">Supplier preparation</p><h1>Questions to ask Chinese suppliers before a factory visit</h1><p class="lead">Send your product specification first. Use these questions to identify who needs to join the meeting, which records to request and what remains unconfirmed.</p><p class="meta">Prepared by ChinaFactoryHost · 15 October 2026</p></header>
<p>These are conversation prompts for a buying decision. For what to observe on the floor, use the <a href="/guides/china-factory-visit-checklist/">factory visit checklist</a>.</p>
<section class="question-group"><h2>Who will make the product?</h2><ul><li>Which company will quote, manufacture and receive payment?</li><li>What is the production address for this product?</li><li>Which processes are done here, and which are handled by another company?</li></ul><p><strong>Request:</strong> the registered company name, the relevant production address and a description of outsourced work. A trading company or subcontractor is not automatically unsuitable; you need to understand responsibility and access.</p><p><strong>If unclear:</strong> clarify the relationships before treating a showroom appointment as a production-site visit. See our <a href="/about/how-we-vet-factories/">supplier checking method</a>.</p></section>
<section class="question-group"><h2>Can the proposed schedule work?</h2><ul><li>What assumptions support the stated capacity: product, line, shifts and yield?</li><li>Which stages would be needed for our specification, including external processes?</li><li>When could samples and production start, and what still needs approval?</li></ul><p><strong>Request:</strong> a schedule tied to your product and order assumptions. An output figure for another product is not a commitment for yours.</p><p><strong>If unclear:</strong> ask the production contact to review the assumptions. Record a response date rather than filling the gap with an estimate.</p></section>
<section class="question-group"><h2>How will quality be agreed and checked?</h2><ul><li>Which specifications and approved samples will production follow?</li><li>What checks are performed during production and before dispatch?</li><li>How are defects, specification changes and corrective actions recorded?</li></ul><p><strong>Request:</strong> relevant examples of records, with customer-confidential information removed where needed. Agree what you may review during the visit.</p><p><strong>If unclear:</strong> identify the evidence or specialist assessment still needed. Seeing equipment or a certificate alone does not establish that your order meets its requirements.</p></section>
<section class="question-group"><h2>Are the quotes comparable?</h2><ul><li>What quantity, specification, packaging and currency does this quote cover?</li><li>What is included or excluded: tooling, samples, testing, freight and other charges?</li><li>What are the MOQ, payment schedule, lead-time assumptions and quote validity?</li><li>Who owns any tooling, and how will access or transfer be agreed?</li></ul><p><strong>Request:</strong> a written quote with the assumptions visible. Put different offers into the <a href="/tools/factory-visit-scorecard/">supplier comparison template</a> before comparing totals.</p><p><strong>If unclear:</strong> request a revised quote for the same specification. Record commercial terms for review; a visit does not replace contract or compliance advice.</p></section>
<section class="note"><h2>End with an owner and a next step</h2><p>Ask: “Which questions remain open, who will answer each one, and by when?” Send a short written recap linking the product specification, quote version, samples and outstanding evidence.</p><p>If the person with authority is unavailable, arrange a follow-up. Do not treat their absence alone as proof that the supplier has failed.</p></section>
